From 72a88ea77c6187ae1a5e199265249365feea8e54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 08:33:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] server: bound slot-action queue wait, and explain empty
 child-slot saves

Two fixes from Dawn's second QA execution pass against the cross-node
KV-cache replication feature (home-infrastructure PR #88, TODO.md
Findings 6 and 8), both root-caused against this fork's own source
before being addressed here.

Finding 6 -- a /slots/N?action=save called immediately after a real
generation sometimes reported n_saved: 0 with only a tiny, fixed-size
n_written (a structurally-valid header, no real KV payload),
indistinguishable at the API level from a genuinely failed or missing
capture.

Root cause, confirmed by direct reproduction against this build: any
slot whose last task was a completed CHILD of an n_cmpl > 1 ("n" in
the OpenAI-compat API) request has its prompt wiped the instant
is_processing flips false, by server_slot::release()'s existing
is_child() branch ("do not keep context of the child slots - the
parent's context is enough"). This is correct, by-design behavior for
that case, not a bug to fix by changing the cleanup -- reproduced live
with n_cmpl=2 against tinyllama2: the parent slot's save reports real
content, the child slot's save reports n_saved: 0 with the same tiny
n_written signature Dawn observed. try_clear_idle_slots(), the other
candidate mechanism identified during research, is ruled out on this
fleet: it is gated on kv_unified, which both of this fleet's production
nodes run with false.

Whether Dawn's own specific repro request used n_cmpl > 1 could not be
confirmed from her recorded evidence (no "n" field mentioned) or from
this fleet's real traffic (no n/n_cmpl handling anywhere in the
LiteLLM integration layer, zero log matches for a literal "n": field
across 7 days / 3M+ lines in both the llamacpp-rpc and litellm log
namespaces). A normal (n_cmpl=1) completion's own save was directly
tested under real --parallel concurrency and a racing unrelated
request and did not reproduce the anomaly. Since the is_child()
mechanism is the only one in the current source capable of producing
Dawn's exact signature, and it is real and live-reproducible, the fix
below is applied regardless of whether it was what she specifically
hit.

Fix: server_task_result_slot_save_load gets a new optional `note`
field, set only when a save's n_saved is legitimately 0 because the
slot's task_prev->is_child() -- explaining the by-design empty state
instead of leaving it silently indistinguishable from a failure.

Finding 8 -- a manual /slots restore against gabesrv10 queued for over
15 minutes behind real concurrent production traffic, with no timeout,
no cancellation path, and no feedback to the caller. Confirmed via
source: SERVER_TASK_TYPE_SLOT_SAVE/RESTORE enter the same FIFO
queue_tasks as ordinary completions with no priority lane, get
deferred if the target slot is busy at dispatch time, and are only
reconsidered on a slot state change -- no existing timeout applies to
a task waiting in that state (-to/--timeout is a socket idle timeout,
inactive while the HTTP handler thread blocks on a condition
variable).

Fix: new `--slot-action-timeout-ms` flag (default 60000; 0 = unbounded,
the prior behavior). handle_slots_save/handle_slots_restore's wait on
the queued task is now bounded by it; on expiry the still-pending task
is cancelled via the existing server_response_reader::stop() path (the
same SERVER_TASK_TYPE_CANCEL / cleanup_pending_task() mechanism already
used on client disconnect) and the caller gets a clear HTTP 503 instead
of an indefinite hang. Does not touch ordinary completion
queueing/dispatch at all. Live-verified: a save against a slot kept
busy by a real generation times out at the configured bound (HTTP 503,
distinct message) while the busy generation itself completes
unaffected.

Out of scope, disclosed rather than silently dropped: queue-position/
ETA visibility for the caller, and a general client-facing
cancel-by-task-ID endpoint for arbitrary tasks. Both are larger
API-surface changes than "don't starve silently forever."

Test coverage added to tools/server/tests/unit/test_slot_save.py:
- a save on the child slot of an n_cmpl=2 request reports n_saved: 0
  with the new note; the parent slot's save is unaffected
- a save immediately after a normal (n_cmpl=1) completion under real
  --parallel 3 concurrency still captures real content (guards against
  the investigated-and-not-reproduced slot-reuse-race hypothesis)
- a save against a slot kept busy past --slot-action-timeout-ms
  returns HTTP 503 promptly instead of hanging, and the unrelated busy
  completion itself is unaffected

tools/server/tests/utils.py gained a slot_action_timeout_ms field to
drive the new flag from the test harness. README.md documents the new
flag and the new `note` response field.

